From 42cecc268af5f2c5bedfd3d3339fd4e742c8f71d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colneech-dev <colneech@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 10:41:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Native floorplanning, congestion feedback, and a
 global-net predicate

Everything here is off unless explicitly enabled, so an unflagged run is
unchanged.

Floorplanning without an external script:

  --floorplan-hierarchy   group cells by RTL scope from yosys hdlname
                          attributes and confine each group to a region
                          derived from its already-placed cells
  --floorplan-pad         padding around each region (default 30)

and apply_region_constraints() reads a floorplan out of the design file
(design attr nextpnr_regions, cell attr REGION), so it can travel in the
JSON rather than needing a --pre-place Python script -- those bindings are
only a build option.

Groups with no anchor are left FREE. An earlier version banded them across
the device so that a design with nothing pre-placed still got regions; that
turned "does nothing" into "fails", because a band sized by group count
confines a cell without reference to where its own BRAMs are.

placer_heap gains a router-fed congestion term (NEXTPNR_CONGESTION_MAP,
NEXTPNR_CONGESTION_W) and a RUDY wire-demand estimate (NEXTPNR_WIRE_DEMAND).
Both are experimental and unmeasured on any real design.

Arch::isGlobalNet() exists because the congestion estimate must exclude nets
on dedicated global routing: they consume no general routing and their
bounding boxes span the die, so counting them buries the real signal.

Caveat for anyone reading the region code as a recommendation: on the design
this was written against, region constraints routed WORSE than no regions in
every geometry tried. It is a mechanism, not a win.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
